## Run locally

```sh
make setup
{{if eq .Recipe.Database.Authority "sqlite"}}
make demo
{{end}}{{if eq .Recipe.Database.Authority "postgres"}}
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost/database make demo
{{end}}
```

The demo starts the service, publishes a boolean flag, evaluates a targeting
match over the OFREP-compatible HTTP surface, and stops the service. A
PostgreSQL demo must use a disposable database; never point it at production.

Run the repository checks:

```sh
make work
make check
```

Checks require Bash, ShellCheck, `rg`, and the selected language toolchain. The
demo additionally requires `curl`, `jq`, and the selected database. Deployment
checks may require the selected provider's CLI.

## Architecture

```text
management HTTP ──┐
                  ▼
                flags ◄──── store/{{.Recipe.Database.Authority}}
                  ▲
evaluation HTTP ──┘
```

Flag validation and evaluation own policy. HTTP and database packages own
mechanism. The composition root is the only place concrete mechanisms meet.

## API surface

The generated service intentionally exposes only:

- `GET /healthz`
- `PUT /v1/environments/{environment}/flags/{key}` — authenticated management publish
- `POST /environments/{environment}/ofrep/v1/evaluate/flags/{key}` — authenticated OFREP single-flag evaluation

Dotted targeting attributes such as `organization.id` are flat evaluation-context
keys on the wire, exactly as OpenFeature providers send them — never nested
objects. Flag listing, audit reading, and OFREP bulk evaluation exist in the
SaaS Reaper golden specimen but are not generated yet. Every publish still
writes an append-only audit row; until an audit endpoint is generated, read the
`audit` table directly in the selected database.

